this lab in new york and others around  the world are racing to answer one of  the most important questions we have  right now  how well will our current vaccines  protect us from omicron that new  worrisome coveted variant as we expected  damacrine is quite resistant so it  evades  detection and inhibition by the  antibodies we produce either after  vaccination or prior infection however  there's also good news a booster it  maintains a level of activity against  omicron that should be sufficient to  provide protection the lab at  rockefeller university is one of the  leaders doing these types of experiments  so i went to get an exclusive look at  the kinds of tests ferologists need to  do to come up with those conclusions and  what the results might mean for us going  forward  when i visited virologists theodora  hatsuyano and paul benosh were about to  finish some key experiments since around  thanksgiving their lab has been working  around the clock on omicron  they've been testing antibodies in  samples from three groups people who  have recovered from covet some of whom  got vaccinated people who have gotten  boosters of pfizer's vaccine and people  who just got a single dose of the j j  shot  the testing involves looking at a key  part of the virus plus patient samples  like this one and then how the  antibodies in those samples interact  with the virus we're basically mimicking  what happens uh in your body when the  virus enters your body and encounters  your antibodies during my visit theodore  showed me a kind of heat map of antibody  virus interactions if it's capable of  inhibiting virus infection you will turn  the red into  light  orange green and even blue if the plasma  is quite powerful  like an alert the more red you see the  less effective antibodies are at  attacking the virus and the more of it  that can get into cells  that's what happened when they tested  omicron the day after my visit they had  the results  some neutralization neutralization or  the ability of antibodies to keep  omicron from entering cells was  generally lower in paul and theodora's  experiments the data corroborates what  corporate and academic labs have found  so far that current vaccines may be less  effective against omicron than previous  versions of the virus theodoras and  other researchers data point to a future  where boosters are the norm and vaccines  get tailored to specific variants  it really means you should get a booster  as soon as possible  and it should ex boosters should be  extended all age categories including  kids so how do the tests that help guide  our vaccine strategy actually work  researchers start by building a  lab-grown version of omicron's spike  protein from scratch the spike protein  is what helps the virus get into cells  it's also a key target for virus busting  antibodies  fabian schmidt is the researcher in  charge of reconstructing the spike  protein he gave me a tour of the  building process starting with an online  database of viral genetic sequences so  you see south african  and then  this is now even allocated omicron  that genetic information serves as the  blueprint for omicron spike the  researchers use bacterial cells to copy  that genetic code  each one of the dots you see here  represents a clone or a specimen of  spike protein dna  the other piece of the puzzle is the  shell of a completely different virus to  which they attach the lab-grown omicron  spike protein these are basically  neutered viruses  they can get into cells but they lack  the machinery to reproduce and spread  that's key because it's what allows the  researchers to measure the extent to  which antibodies can stop the spike  protein from getting these fake viruses  to latch onto and infect cells  in a separate and secured section of the  lab the researchers incubate their fake  virus with patient samples from  vaccinated people and people who've had  coveted that liquid has antibodies  the virus antibody mixture is then added  to cells that have been sitting in a  warm incubator inside these little black  containers  critically these cells have the receptor  that the spike protein binds to allowing  the virus to enter if the neutered virus  gets in it varies a set of instructions  that tells the cells to make luciferase  that's a protein that glows and is  measured by the instrument theodora  showed us earlier  more glow means more infection which is  shown in red that means the antibodies  were less effective  they compared how well antibodies  prevented omicron spike from helping the  virus get into cells relative to the  original version of spike and they found  that the antibodies in our body  particularly over time appear really  poor at uh dealing with omicron the  samples they tested from patients  spanned several months from right after  they were vaccinated or infected to  months later and we know that antibody  levels tend to wane with time plus none  of these people were infected with  omicron when people got boosters though  their antibody levels went up and still  retained sufficient activity to make us  hopeful that these people will be able  to deal with omicron infections  the adora said that the extra antibodies  that boosters provide should help  protect us against the most severe  outcomes including hospitalization i  still remain worried that irmicron will  transmit between vaccinated people  easier so we still need to have masks  and social distancing for a little while  longer in the coming weeks we should  have more definitive answers about  omicron's real world impact from data on  how well vaccines protect people not  just cells  public health experts will be able to  see whether vaccinated people are  getting more breakthrough infections  those have tended to be milder but what  is really driving evolution and  emergence of the variants is resistant  to antibody responses  theodora another said that vaccination  rates need to increase to give the virus  fewer chances to infect mutate and pose  new threats in the future  [Music]
 

